\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

Aggregate accuracy masks the fact that adjacent depths become harder to distinguish as depth increases.
We quantify this via the \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} (EDD).
For each adjacent depth pair $(d, d{+}1)$, we train a dedicated binary RF (500 trees, 5-fold stratified CV) and compute $\mathrm{AUC}(d, d{+}1)$---the ROC AUC of this pairwise classifier at separating depth-$d$ from depth-$(d{+}1)$ samples.
Given a discrimination threshold $T$, EDD is defined as:
\[
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 : \mathrm{AUC}(d^*, d^*{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\]
where $d^* \in \{0, 1, \dots, K{-}1\}$.
Intuitively, $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ is the deepest generation depth at which the classifier can still reliably separate it from the preceding depth; beyond this boundary, adjacent depths become statistically indistinguishable.
We set $T{=}0.75$ as the default threshold---the midpoint between chance AUC (0.5) and perfect discrimination (1.0)---and report sensitivity at $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$ below.

Within the 4-class atlas (d0--d3), 7 of 9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=3$; the two exceptions are Pythia and OLMo with top-$k$ sampling ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=2$), consistent with top-$k$'s signal-attenuating effect noted in \S\ref{sec:atlas:indist}.

To probe deeper, we extend the Pythia-1.4B nucleus pipeline to 6 depths (d0--d5) and train a 500-tree binary RF for each adjacent pair (we increase from 200 to 500 trees for binary classifiers to ensure convergence on the smaller per-pair sample sizes).
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} shows the pairwise AUC decay curve.
The d0\,vs.\,d1 boundary is near-perfect (AUC\,=\,0.998), reflecting the well-known human--machine gap.
Subsequent boundaries degrade monotonically: d1\,vs.\,d2 achieves 0.925 [95\% CI: 0.914,\,0.935], d2\,vs.\,d3 reaches 0.823 [0.805,\,0.840], and d3\,vs.\,d4 yields 0.784 [0.765,\,0.802]---with the confidence interval lower bound still exceeding $T{=}0.75$.
At d4\,vs.\,d5, AUC drops to 0.683 [0.661,\,0.705], falling below the threshold and fixing $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=4$.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC between adjacent depths (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus $p{=}0.95$, d0--d5). Shaded bands: 95\% bootstrap CI. Dashed line: $T{=}0.75$. $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=4$: depth pairs up to d3\,vs.\,d4 remain discriminable.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity} shows the sensitivity of EDD to the choice of threshold.
At $T{=}0.65$, all pairs through d4\,vs.\,d5 pass, giving $\mathrm{EDD}=5$.
At $T{=}0.80$, only d1\,vs.\,d2 and d2\,vs.\,d3 pass, yielding $\mathrm{EDD}=3$.
The d0\,vs.\,d1 pair passes at every threshold ($\text{AUC}\geq0.99$).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{EDD sensitivity to threshold $T$ (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus $p{=}0.95$). \checkmark: pairwise AUC $\geq T$; ---: below $T$. d0\,vs.\,d1 always passes (AUC$\geq$0.99) and is omitted.}
\label{tab:edd_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{cccccr}
\toprule
$T$ & d1/d2 & d2/d3 & d3/d4 & d4/d5 & EDD \\
\midrule
0.65 & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & 5 \\
0.70 & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & --- & 4 \\
0.75 & \checkmark & \checkmark & \checkmark & --- & 4 \\
0.80 & \checkmark & \checkmark & --- & --- & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Granularity ablation.}
Decision boundary dilution motivates EDD's binary-pair design: mean accuracy drops from 99.5\% (binary) to 78.3\% (4-class) to 62.9\% (6-class), and the d2\,vs.\,d3 AUC falls from 0.804 to 0.689 between the 4-class and 6-class settings for Pythia nucleus---a 14\% relative decline attributable to boundary dilution, not signal loss.

\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Trajectories}
\label{sec:atlas:trajectories}

The atlas reveals that decoding strategy does not merely scale the overall signal strength---it reshapes the degradation trajectory.
Figure~\ref{fig:feature_trajectory} plots normalized feature means across depths for three key features.

\paragraph{Temperature-induced boundary inversion.}
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature yields d1v2 AUC of 0.129 for GPT-2~XL and 0.253 for OLMo---both below 0.5, indicating that the d1/d2 decision boundary \emph{inverts} (Pythia retains a degraded 0.576).
This follows from the trajectory analysis in \S\ref{sec:atlas}: temperature sampling shifts feature values at depth $d$ toward depth $d{+}1$ under nucleus/top-$k$.

\paragraph{Z-score normalization does not resolve the inversion.}
A natural hypothesis is that per-feature z-score normalization might correct this covariate shift \cite{sugiyama2007_importance_weighting}.
For RF, z-normalization leaves accuracy essentially unchanged (59.1\% $\to$ 59.2\%) and does not improve d1v2 AUC ($\Delta < 0.02$), as expected since tree splits depend on feature rank order, not scale.
Z-score standardization improves overall logistic regression cross-strategy accuracy from 25\% to 61\%, with d1v2 AUC gains for nucleus and top-$k$ holdouts (0.61--0.89).
However, it does not recover the temperature-induced d1v2 inversion---AUC worsens for two of three models (GPT-2~XL 0.50$\to$0.24, OLMo 0.50$\to$0.19; Pythia 0.52), confirming that temperature decoding induces a genuine geometry-level shift in feature space rather than a correctable scale difference.

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:dcol}

To test whether pretrained language models can learn depth signals directly from raw text, we finetune DeBERTa-v3-base and RoBERTa-base end-to-end (lr$\,{=}\,2{\times}10^{-5}$, 3 epochs, 5-fold CV) on Pythia-1.4B nucleus data.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Three-tier strategy taxonomy.}
Cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) reveals that decoding strategies form three behaviorally distinct tiers for depth estimation, rather than a single ``soft sampling'' category:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Nucleus} ($p{=}0.95$): Baseline-stable.
  Consistent depth signal across models; no cross-strategy anomalies.
\item \textbf{Temperature} ($\tau{=}0.9$): Signal enhancement with boundary reversal risk.
  In-distribution accuracy improves for GPT-2~XL ($+$2.1pp) and OLMo ($+$3.3pp), but cross-strategy holdout yields d1v2 AUC as low as 0.129 (Table~\ref{tab:d1v2_strategy}), indicating a \emph{reversed} d1/d2 decision boundary.
\item \textbf{Top-$k$} ($k{=}50$): Signal attenuation, direction preserved.
  Pythia top-$k$ accuracy drops 8.6pp below nucleus (69.6\% vs.\ 78.2\%), but ordinal direction is maintained across all models.
\end{itemize}

\noindent Table~\ref{tab:d1v2_strategy} quantifies this pattern through pairwise d1v2 AUC.
In-distribution, all strategies achieve strong d1/d2 discrimination (AUC$\,{\geq}\,$0.76).
Under cross-strategy transfer (train on nucleus$+$top-$k$, test on temperature), GPT-2~XL and OLMo d1v2 AUC drops below 0.5---the classifier systematically confuses depth~1 for depth~2 and vice versa.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pairwise d1v2 AUC across decoding strategies. First three columns: in-distribution (5-fold CV). Last column: cross-strategy holdout (train on nucleus$+$top-$k$, test on temperature). Values below 0.5 indicate boundary inversion.}
\label{tab:d1v2_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{In-distribution d1v2 AUC} & Temp09 \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4}
Model & Nucleus & Temp09 & Top-$k$ & holdout \\
\midrule
Pythia & 0.868 & 0.835 & 0.761 & 0.576 \\
GPT-2 XL & 0.916 & 0.939 & 0.824 & \textbf{0.129} \\
OLMo & 0.870 & 0.903 & 0.831 & \textbf{0.253} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Accelerated convergence mechanism.}
The inversion arises because temperature uniformly scales all logits before softmax, whereas nucleus and top-$k$ truncate the distribution tail.
Uniform scaling accelerates convergence toward the attractor distribution \cite{shumailov2024_nature}: temperature depth~$N$ features approximate nucleus/top-$k$ depth~$N{+}1$ features (e.g., GPT-2~XL temp09 d1 \textsc{p90\_ppl} median${=}$92.2 falls into the nucleus/top-$k$ d2 range of 65--92).
Z-score normalization improves overall logistic regression cross-strategy accuracy ($25\%{\to}61\%$) but does not recover pairwise d1v2 AUC---GPT-2~XL worsens from 0.50 to 0.24 and OLMo from 0.50 to 0.19 (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy})---confirming a genuine geometry-level distributional shift rather than a correctable scale artifact.

\paragraph{Depth-graduated data curation.}
Predicted depth can serve as a continuous quality proxy for importance weighting in curation pipelines \cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}, retaining shallow-generation text while down-weighting deeply recursive samples that binary filters \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse} would indiscriminately discard.
Feature importance shifts with depth, aligning with tail erosion theory \cite{shumailov2024_nature}.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced recursive generation depth estimation and showed that 19 lightweight statistical features---with p90 perplexity and surprisal variance as universal markers---achieve 70--85\% four-class accuracy (chance = 25\%) with $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$.
Cross-model transfer reaches 66.4\% and cross-strategy 59.1\%, though the temperature-induced d1/d2 inversion and PLMs' ${\sim}$59\% ceiling underscore remaining challenges; future work should address wild-data transfer, larger scales, and strategy-conditioned classifiers.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment constraint.}
Deploying depth estimators across decoding strategies requires knowing the target strategy type a priori.
Without this knowledge, d1/d2 predictions may systematically invert: cross-strategy holdout d1v2 AUC drops to 0.129 (GPT-2~XL) and 0.253 (OLMo) when temperature-sampled text is classified by a model trained on nucleus and top-$k$ (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}).
Strategy-conditioned classifiers or automatic strategy identification are needed before deployment.

\paragraph{d0 inflation of headline metrics.}
Depth~0 (human-written text) is near-perfectly separable (recall $>$98\% across all settings), inflating headline accuracy.
Cross-model transfer accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially driven by d0; d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\paragraph{Controlled vs.\ ecological setting.}
All experiments use Wikipedia seed text with controlled recursive generation (LoRA fine-tuning, 1--1.5B parameter models).
Transfer to mixed-domain corpora, instruction-tuned models, and larger scales (7B+) remains open.
Wild-data calibration is probed only indirectly; ecological transfer is future work.

\paragraph{EDD threshold sensitivity.}
The Effective Discriminable Depth depends on the AUC threshold $T$: at $T{=}0.80$, several cells lose one discriminable depth level relative to $T{=}0.75$.
The choice of $T$ is application-dependent and affects reported EDD values.

\paragraph{Reference model dependency.}
Surprisal-based features are computed relative to GPT-2 base (124M), introducing partial overlap when GPT-2~XL is held out, though GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed the unrelated OLMo (69.2\%).
The choice of reference model affects feature values; a different reference model may shift absolute feature magnitudes and potentially alter discriminability at depth boundaries.

\paragraph{Additional caveats.}
Detection features degrade under domain shift \cite{pudasaini2026_detection_fails}; our depth features are likely susceptible.
\citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} find that early recursion can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality, potentially violating the monotonicity assumption, and \citet{falahati2026_curated} show that curated synthetic data need not trigger collapse, attenuating the fingerprints we exploit.

\section{The Depth Degradation Atlas}
\label{sec:atlas}

We systematically characterize how recursive generation reshapes the statistical profile of text across a grid of 3 model families $\times$ 3 decoding strategies $\times$ 4 generation depths (d0--d3), yielding 9 experimental configurations each spanning 4 depths.
For each cell we train a Random Forest (RF) classifier on the 19 statistical features defined in \S\ref{sec:method} and examine (i)~in-distribution depth classification accuracy, (ii)~which features carry depth signal, (iii)~how far into the recursion chain depth remains discriminable, and (iv)~how decoding strategy modulates the degradation trajectory.

\subsection{In-Distribution Performance}
\label{sec:atlas:indist}

Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} reports 4-class depth estimation accuracy under 5-fold cross-validation.
All 9 cells exceed chance (25\%) by a wide margin, with accuracy ranging from 69.6\% to 85.4\%.
Both model family and decoding strategy influence accuracy, with model ordering GPT-2~XL $>$ OLMo $>$ Pythia holding across all strategies.
Top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) trails by 6--9 percentage points, while temperature and nucleus sampling alternate for the lead depending on model family.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{In-distribution 4-class depth estimation accuracy (\%) across 3 model families $\times$ 3 decoding strategies. RF classifier with 19 statistical features, 5-fold CV (std 0.2--0.9pp across all cells). Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:main_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Model & Nucleus & Temp & Top-$k$ \\
 & $p{=}0.95$ & $\tau{=}0.9$ & $k{=}50$ \\
\midrule
Pythia-1.4B & 78.2 & 76.3 & 69.6 \\
OLMo-1B & 78.8 & 82.0 & 74.2 \\
GPT-2 XL & 83.3 & \textbf{85.4} & 76.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Because the human-written seed text (d0) is often trivially separable from any machine-generated depth, we also evaluate 3-class accuracy excluding d0.
Performance drops moderately---GPT-2~XL with temperature sampling still reaches 82.0\%, and even the weakest setting (Pythia nucleus) retains 70.8\%---confirming that the classifier captures inter-generation differences beyond the human--machine boundary.

\subsection{Feature Universality}
\label{sec:atlas:features}

To identify which statistical fingerprints carry depth signal, we train a dedicated binary RF per cell (d$_i$ vs.\ rest) and extract feature importances (Gini impurity; permutation importance yields consistent rankings, Appendix~\ref{app:permutation}).
Figure~\ref{fig:atlas_heatmap} visualizes the top-7 feature importances across all 9 cells.

Two features dominate.
The 90th-percentile perplexity (\textsc{p90\_ppl}) ranks in the top 2 in all 9 cells, while surprisal variance (\textsc{var\_surprisal}) does so in 7 of 9.
In the two exceptions---GPT-2~XL with temperature and top-$k$ sampling---the 75th-percentile perplexity (\textsc{p75\_ppl}) replaces surprisal variance in the top 2, though surprisal variance remains among the top 4 features.
To quantify cross-model agreement, we compute the Spearman rank correlation of the full 19-feature importance vectors across all $\binom{9}{2}=36$ cell pairs and obtain a mean $\rho = 0.868$ (range: 0.619--0.954).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_atlas_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Feature importance heatmap across 9 cells (3 models $\times$ 3 strategies). Top-7 features by mean importance. \textsc{p90\_ppl} appears in the top 2 for all cells; \textsc{var\_surprisal} for 7 of 9.}
\label{fig:atlas_heatmap}
\end{figure}

These results align with theoretical predictions: recursive finetuning erodes the tail of the token-probability distribution \cite{shumailov2024_nature,dohmatob2024_tale_of_tails}, and high-percentile perplexity directly measures tail behavior.
The universality of these markers across architectures and decoding strategies suggests they reflect a structural property of the recursive generation process rather than model-specific artifacts.

\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:atlas:edd}

Aggregate accuracy masks that adjacent depths become harder to distinguish as depth increases; we quantify this via the \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} (EDD).
For each adjacent depth pair $(d, d{+}1)$, we train a dedicated binary RF (500 trees, 5-fold stratified CV) and compute $\mathrm{AUC}(d, d{+}1)$---the ROC AUC of this pairwise classifier at separating depth-$d$ from depth-$(d{+}1)$ samples.
Given a discrimination threshold $T$, EDD is defined as:
\[
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 : \mathrm{AUC}(d^*, d^*{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\]
where $d^* \in \{0, 1, \dots, K{-}1\}$.
Intuitively, $\mathrm{EDD}_T$ is the deepest generation depth at which the classifier can still reliably separate it from the preceding depth; beyond this boundary, adjacent depths become statistically indistinguishable.
We set $T{=}0.75$ as the default threshold---the midpoint between chance AUC (0.5) and perfect discrimination (1.0)---and report sensitivity at $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$ below.

Within the 4-class atlas (d0--d3), 7 of 9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=3$; the two exceptions are Pythia and OLMo with top-$k$ sampling ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=2$), consistent with top-$k$'s signal-attenuating effect noted in \S\ref{sec:atlas:indist}.

We extend the Pythia-1.4B nucleus pipeline to 6 depths (d0--d5) and train a 500-tree binary RF for each adjacent pair.
Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} shows the pairwise AUC decay curve.
The d0\,vs.\,d1 boundary is near-perfect (AUC\,=\,0.998), reflecting the well-known human--machine gap.
Subsequent boundaries degrade monotonically: d1\,vs.\,d2 achieves 0.925 [95\% CI: 0.914,\,0.935], d2\,vs.\,d3 reaches 0.823 [0.805,\,0.840], and d3\,vs.\,d4 yields 0.784 [0.765,\,0.802]---with the confidence interval lower bound still exceeding $T{=}0.75$.
At d4\,vs.\,d5, AUC drops to 0.683 [0.661,\,0.705], falling below the threshold and fixing $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=4$.

\paragraph{Cross-model EDD.}
We extend the same d0--d5 pipeline to OLMo-1B and GPT-2~XL (both nucleus $p{=}0.95$).
The d3\,vs.\,d4 AUC consistently clusters near the 0.75 threshold: Pythia 0.784 ($p{=}0.001$), OLMo 0.765 ($p{=}0.091$, marginal), GPT-2~XL 0.750 ($p{=}0.505$, n.s.)---only Pythia achieves conventional significance, yet all three exhibit the same directional pattern.
At d4\,vs.\,d5, AUC uniformly drops below 0.70 (Pythia 0.683, OLMo 0.694, GPT-2~XL 0.681), fixing $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$ for all three models.
This cross-model EDD is stable across threshold choices: all three yield $\mathrm{EDD}{=}5$ at $T{=}0.65$, $\mathrm{EDD}{=}4$ at $T \in \{0.70, 0.75\}$, and $\mathrm{EDD}{=}3$ at $T{=}0.80$, matching the single-model pattern in Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Binary pairwise AUC between adjacent depths (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus $p{=}0.95$, d0--d5). Shaded bands: 95\% bootstrap CI. Dashed line: $T{=}0.75$. $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}=4$: depth pairs up to d3\,vs.\,d4 remain discriminable.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:edd_sensitivity} shows the sensitivity of EDD to the choice of threshold.
At $T{=}0.65$, all pairs through d4\,vs.\,d5 pass, giving $\mathrm{EDD}=5$.
At $T{=}0.80$, only d1\,vs.\,d2 and d2\,vs.\,d3 pass, yielding $\mathrm{EDD}=3$.
The d0\,vs.\,d1 pair passes at every threshold ($\text{AUC}\geq0.99$).

